  "scope": "Complete unchanged JewelleryCraftPlugin, JewelleryCrafting (with JewelleryItem), JewelleryPulse, JewelleryInterface, core.api.InputType, Pulse, SkillPulse, NodeUsageEvent, Plugin and Initializable, compiled with Kotlin 1.8.20/JDK 11 against the pinned ConstLib 1.4.28 artifact. ContentAPI's Boolean sendInputDialogue, StringUtils.isPlusN and Animation's two short constructors and create are pinned text spliced verbatim. Player, Item, the inventory (a map of amounts: contains is true when at least the amount is held, also for an amount of zero or less; remove(Item...) takes each item of which the amount is held and answers whether every one was taken; add always succeeds), the level, the interface manager's open and close, sendMessage, Player.sendMessages, sendInterfaceConfig, sendItemZoomOnInterface, playAudio, the InputType sendInputDialogue (which records the type and the prompt and keeps the handler), Skills.addExperience, SlayerManager's ring flag and PulseManager.run (which records the pulse's fields as constructed, calls its start() as the original manager does and keeps it) are recording adapters whose results come from declared case state; they are service inputs, not ports. ItemDefinition is an adapter whose names are the corpus's declared table (oracle/crafting-jewellery/cases.py) or a case's own name or null: they are not read from the cache, and the branches of open() and handle() that depend on a name are covered for those names only. UseWithHandler is an adapter with the type constants, the allowed nodes and a recorded addHandler: its run, its lookup by key and its pulses are not covered. ComponentDefinition.put records what is put. The Skills constants are pinned text spliced into an adapter shell. The driver calls JewelleryCraftPlugin.handle with an event of two items it names, JewelleryInterface.handle with a component 446, an opcode and a button it names and slot and item 0, the last amount prompt's handler with an Integer or a String, and pulse() and stop() of the pulse the last run kept, not Pulse.update(): nothing here establishes on which ticks a pulse runs, that a finished pulse is stopped, or how PulseManager replaces a running pulse. No live world, original Container/Skills arithmetic, interface dispatch, input prompt, slayer or item definition behaviour is established by this corpus.",
